<div class="topic-sheet">
  <h1>Noun Phrases - What they are</h1>
  <p>
    A <strong>noun</strong> names a person, place, thing or idea: <em>dog, mountain, book, courage</em>. A <strong>noun phrase</strong> is a group of words built around a noun. The main noun is the <strong>head noun</strong>. Adding useful detail changes what the reader pictures, and which thing they think you mean.
  </p>
  <section class="summary-block summary-peach">
    <h2>Find the head noun</h2>
    <p>In “<strong>the dog</strong> barked,” <em>dog</em> is the head noun. <em>The dog</em> is the noun phrase.</p>
    <p>
      In “<strong>The small dog with a red collar</strong> slept,” the bold words are the subject noun
      phrase. <em>Slept</em> is the verb outside it.
    </p>
  </section>
  <section class="summary-block summary-yellow">
    <h2>An adjective is not always inside the noun phrase</h2>
    <p>An adjective can sit before the noun: the <strong>ancient</strong> tree, a <strong>narrow</strong> lane.</p>
    <p>
      It can also follow a linking verb: “The tree was <strong>crooked</strong>.” <em>Crooked</em> still
      describes the tree, but it is not part of the subject noun phrase “The tree.”
    </p>
    <p>When you mark a noun phrase, stop at its boundary. Do not include every word that describes the subject.</p>
  </section>
  <section class="summary-block summary-green">
    <h2>Worked example</h2>
    <p><strong>The huge, ancient tree beside the church</strong> fell.</p>
    <p>Head noun: <strong>tree</strong>. <em>Huge</em> and <em>ancient</em> are adjectives.</p>
    <p><em>Beside the church</em> is a prepositional phrase attached to <em>tree</em>. The verb <em>fell</em> is outside the noun phrase.</p>
  </section>
</div>
